.nr % 1
.th OLD I 1/1/76
.sh NAME
old \*- overlay linker
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd old
[ overlay description ]
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Old
is an overlay link-editor for UNIX-style object files.
It is designed to allow the structuring of
logically closely related programs which are too large to be entirely core-resident. The overlaying process can be 
made automatic, and completely transparent, to C-language programs.
.s3
Programs to be overlaid must be capable of having an n-ary tree description of their calling
sequence. The head node of the tree is the resident section; it is composed of the
.bd root
and
.bd upool
segments. All other nodes of the tree are overlay segments.
.s3
A segment may reference any symbols in itself, an ancestor, or a descendant.
This definition allows multiple definitions of global symbols in distinct paths, provided they are
not ambiguous.
Common blocks are automatically located in the most ancestral node in which they appear; again,
non-ambiguous multiple definitions are permitted. Common blocks of initial zero length may be placed in
a node by means of the "-p" construction in a segment description: see below.
.s3
After the
.bd root
and all overlay segments are searched, remaining undefined references are
duplicated in the
.bd upool.
The
.bd upool
is then searched; it is assumed that this will 
commonly be used to force frequently used library routines into the resident section.
.s3
A library module is included iff it defines some undefined symbol which has not been "excluded."
.s3
Segments are assigned core address in such a way that a segment and all of its ancestors can be simultaneously resident.
.s3
The overlay linker defines the following global symbols in the data portion of the resident section
(all symbol names should have an additional "_" prepended from assemly language) :
.s3
.nf
	_o_name
	_o_segs
	_o_sege
.fi
.s3
and the following symbol in the bss portion of the resident section:
.s3
	_o_oreg
.s3
If any symbols have been declared as "autoloads," the symbol
.s3
	_o_aut
.s3
is made undefined in the root segment.
.s3
"_o_name" is the address of the first byte of a null-terminated UNIX pathname;
this pathname can be given either in the link description via the
"-o" option, or assigned the default "ov.out."
The run-time overlay handler assumes that the pathname is the name of the overlay file.
.s3
"_o_segs" points to the first word of the overlay segment tables, and "_o_sege"
points to the first word following the last segment table. Segment tables have the following format:
.s3
.nf
	[byte offset for start of segment	]
	[block offset for start of segment	]
	[byte count for segment read		]
	[segment low core address		]
	[segment high core address		]
	[segment number				]
.fi
.s3
Each autoload entry also has a unique autoload vector of the form
.s3
.nf
	[jsr r4,*$			]
	[  _o_aut			]
	[segment table address of entry	]
	[core address of entry		]
.fi
.s3
"_o_oreg" is the address of the overlay region.
.s3
If a symbol is declared "autoload," intra-segmental references to that symbol refer directly to
it. Extra-segmental references are converted to references to that entry's autoload vector alias. These references must
occur in transfers of control directly to the symbol, and will force loading of the segment if it is not already resident.
.s3
A system library routine is available to define "_o_aut," and perform several other
overlay-related functions.
A C-language call is available to force manual loading of a segment:
.s3
.lp +20 15
_o_segl(n)	An attempt is made to load segment n. If segment n is found, a pointer to its
segment table is returned. Otherwise, -1 is returned.
.i0
.dt
.s3
The external character-pointer cell "_o_file" originally points to "_o_name"
This may be changed before any overlay activity has occured to point to the pathname of the
desired overlay file.
.s3
The format of the "a.out" file is as described in the UNIX manual.
The "ov.out" contains core images of the text and data portions of the overlay
segments, and is indexed by the segment tables.
The "ov.sym" file has the format:
.ne 15
.s3
.nf
	n = # of segments (overlay)
		# of symbols in segment 1
		# of symbols in segment 2
			...
		# of symbols in segment n
.s3
	symbols for segment 1
		...
	symbols for segment n
.fi
.s3
The order of the segments is as in "ov.out."
.s3
The core map of an overlay program is as follows:
.ne 15
.nf
.s3
		resident text region
		resident data region
			user defined data
	_o_name ->	null-terminated path name
	_o_segs ->	segment tables
	_o_sege ->	autoload vectors
.s3
		resident bss region
	_o_oreg ->	overlay region
			resident common
			resident bss
.fi
.s3
.s3
If it is not otherwise defined, "end" points to the first word after the resident bss region.
The symbols "etext" and "edata," defined in a similar manner in
.it ld
(I), are omitted, because
their definition in an overlay program is problematical.
.s3
The syntax the overlay loader accepts is composed of the following:
.s3
.lp +14 12
-d fn	Input is diverted from the named file until it is exhausted.
.s3
.lp +14 12
-o on	The pathname located at "_o_name" is "on". the default is "ov.out"
.s3
.lp +14 12
-xn	The degree of symbolic output in the resident section is controlled as follows:
.s3
.nf
		n = 0: no symbolic output (default)
		n = 1: global symbols only
		n = 2: global symbols and non-C-style locals
		n = 3: all symbols
.fi
.s3
.lp +14 12
-X	Equivalent to -x2
.s3
.lp +14 12
-yn	The degree of symbolic output in the "ov.sym" file is controlled as follows:
.s3
.nf
		n = 0:	no "ov.sym" file (default)
		n = 1:	global symbols only
		n = 2:	global symbols and non-C-style locals
		n = 3:	all symbols
.fi
.s3
.lp +14 12
-Y	Equivalent to -y2
.s3
.lp +14 12
-n	The text and data segments are separated. The text segment is write-protected at run-time.
.s3
.lp +14 12
-m	A load map is produced to the standard output
.s3
.lp +14 12
-R	A description of the
.bd root
segment follows (the
.bd root
is referred to as segment -1 in
the load map and diagnostics)
.s3
.lp +14 12
-U	A description of the
.bd upool
segment follows (the
.bd upool
is referred to as segment -2 in the
load map and diagnostics)
.s3
.lp +14 12
-S n	A description of segment n (1 <_ n <_ 32767) follows.
.i0
.dt
.s3
.ul
Segment descriptors
.s3
The initial items are the filenames of object files to comprise the segment.
The following abbreviations are recognized:
.s3
.nf
		"-l?" for "/lib/lib?.a"
		"-l" for "/lib/liba.a"
.fi
.s3
After the filename, any or all of the following may appear in any order:
.s3
.lp +16 14
-c c1 ... cn	The segment calls segments c1 ... cn
.s3
.lp +16 14
-p p1 ... pn	Common blocks p1 ... pn are placed in the segment with an initial length of 0.
.s3
.lp +16 14
-a a1 ... an	The symbols a1 ... an are declared to be autoload
.i0
.dt
.s3
The following may appear with the file names:
.s3
.lp +16 14
-i sm	The symbol "sm" is made a candidate for library inclusion. If it was previously excluded, the
exclusion is annulled.
.s3
.lp +16 14
-e sm	The symbols "sm" is excluded from consideration for library inclusion.
.i0
.dt
.s3
.ul
Example
.s3
The linker is linked as follows:
.s3
		%old -m -d lfile >link_map
.s3
"lfile" contains:
.s3
.nf
		-R /lib/crt0.o main.o find.o printf.o -c 1 2 3 4 5
			-U -lc -l
			-S 1 parse.o	-a	_parse
			-S 2 link1.o	-a	_link1
			-S 3 address.o	-a	_address
			-S 4 map.o	-a	_map
			-S 5 link2.o	-a	_link2
.fi
.sh BUGS
